# zero-vault-pg configuration
#
# This example deliberately keeps NO DB_* keys here. The Postgres connection
# config (DB_HOST/DB_PORT/DB_USER/DB_PASSWORD/DB_NAME/DB_DIALECT/DB_SSL_MODE) is
# injected from HashiCorp Vault at startup by zero's built-in vault loader.
#
# zero calls vault.load() during App startup (before datasources are wired) when
# VAULT_ADDR is set. It authenticates with VAULT_TOKEN, or with AppRole via
# VAULT_ROLE_ID + VAULT_SECRET_ID, then merges every key from the comma-separated
# VAULT_SECRETS_PATH into the process config, so ctx.SQL binds Postgres from vault
# secrets. DB_SSL_MODE=disable (the default) gives a plaintext, no-TLS link.
#
# VAULT_SECRETS_PATH may use either KV v1 form (secret/zero/db) or the KV v2
# form (secret/data/zero/db) — zero inserts the `data/` segment automatically on
# a 404, matching `vault kv` CLI behaviour.

# Where the Vault server listens.
VAULT_ADDR=http://127.0.0.1:8200

# KV secret path(s) holding the DB_* connection keys, comma-separated.
# (This dev Vault's KV v2 mount is `secret`; the secret is named `zero/db`.)
VAULT_SECRETS_PATH=secret/data/zero/db

# VAULT_TOKEN (or VAULT_ROLE_ID + VAULT_SECRET_ID) is supplied at runtime, NOT
# committed here. For the Vault dev container used with this example the token is
# the literal string "token", so run: VAULT_TOKEN=token ./zig-out/bin/vaultpg
# A bad token surfaces a clear "HTTP 403" error from the loader — fix the token.
# VAULT_TOKEN=token
